  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two stages in microbiome-based agriculture. On the left, the “first generation” approach is simple microbial application: selected beneficial microorganisms are added directly to plants, shown as a set of bacteria-like shapes being applied to a crop root system. The text suggests this includes single strains, synthetic communities, and broader microbiome restoration approaches.\n\nThe right side presents a more advanced “second generation” strategy focused on shaping the whole plant microbiome rather than just adding a few microbes. This includes plant breeding and stimulation, illustrated with DNA, chemical structures, phages, and farm machinery, implying the use of genetics, compounds, biological agents, and agricultural practices to direct microbiome assembly. It also includes microbiome stimulation, shown by diverse microbial communities and analytical profiling, suggesting monitoring and steering existing microbial populations.\n\nAcross the bottom, a series of plants represents selection over many varieties or generations, leading to plants optimized for stronger plant–microbe interactions. Overall, the figure shows a shift from direct inoculation of microbes toward targeted, knowledge-driven modulation of microbiomes and breeding plants that can better recruit and maintain beneficial microbial communities.",
